september is suicide prevention  awareness month for many people in  communities of color the process of  navigating treatment can be quite  difficult mary jane bellies joins us in  studio now to explain mary jane  local organizations want to bridge that  gap and break barriers to minority  mental health care but they say it's  going to take the community's help  racial and ethnic minority communities  still face unique struggles when it  comes to mental health dr sid carrana  from nevada mental health knows the  struggles minorities face all too well  asian asian culture the culture i come  from  we are one third less likely to seek  mental health care than the caucasian  counterparts and that comes from our own  perceptions of mental illness lack of  adequate insight in many cultures the  negative stigma about mental health  comes from fear or shame preventing  people from seeking help they do not  believe that  they will get the right care from their  treatment providers that their culture  would be understood their language would  be understood they they have a distrust  of the system and that some many times  are factors keeping them from seeking  care other factors also include the type  of community you live in lack of  transportation and lack of health  insurance dr sheldon jacobs the vice  president of nami the national alliance  of mental illness in southern nevada is  trying to change that we've  been working a little bit more with  youth as well as communities of color  and so really one of the emphasis has  been on just meeting people where they  are dr jacobs says nami is working on  partnering with other local mental  health organizations to identify those  needs so really trying to  educate  you know individuals and various  communities about mental health and then  also just trying to normalize as much as  possible by  by spreading awareness  nami has a number of services like  peer-led support groups and mental  health events free of charge  thank you mary jane  the next event called sharing hope a  night of exploring mental wellness  within the black community will take  place at city hall september 23 at 5 30.
 

